StFstDbMaker/macro -- FST geometry (survey/alignment) tables
============================================================

What is here
------------
  fst/*.C                 the Geometry/fst survey tables, one file per
                          (table, beginTime) pair, in St_db_Maker's
                          <table>.<yyyymmdd>.<hhmmss>.C naming
  fstGeometry_db.C        reads those files and pushes them to the offline
                          MySQL DB (see "Pushing to the database" below)

These four tables are chained by StFstDb::loadGeometry() to place every FST
sensor:

      fstOnTpc            FST global position inside the TPC frame   (1 row)
      hssOnFst            each of the 2 half-support structures      (2 rows)
      fstWedgeOnHss       each wedge on its HSS                      (36 rows)
      fstSensorOnWedge    each sensor on its wedge                   (108 rows)

The DB is the truth. These files are the record of what was pushed, and the
way to override it locally.


Using these tables locally instead of the database
--------------------------------------------------
To MODIFY the geometry -- try an alignment, test a shift, reproduce an old
iteration -- do NOT edit the database. Put the tables in your working directory
and tell the chain to look only there.

  THE EASY WAY: force local-only with two bfc options.

      NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb

  NoMySQLDb drops MySQL and NoStarCintDb drops $STAR/StarDb, leaving your own
  $PWD/StarDb as the only place tables come from. Your files then win outright
  and you never have to think about timestamps. Set-up:

      mkdir -p ./StarDb/Geometry
      cp -r $STAR/StRoot/StFstDbMaker/macro/fst ./StarDb/Geometry/fst
      # or: ln -s $STAR/StRoot/StFstDbMaker/macro/fst ./StarDb/Geometry/fst

  then run the chain with NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb among its options. This is the
  right mode for alignment work and for any test of local table files.

  The four options, from BigFullChain.h:1159 (StBFChain.cxx:263-268 empties the
  matching directory out of the list):

      NoMySQLDb        drop MySQL
      NoStarCintDb     drop $STAR/StarDb
      NoLocalCintDb    drop $PWD/StarDb
      NoCintDb         drop BOTH $STAR/StarDb and $PWD/StarDb -> MySQL only

  Note NoStarCintDb does not clear $STAR/.$STAR_HOST_SYS/obj/StarDb, which
  stays in the list. That is harmless for us today -- the release has no
  Geometry/ tables there -- but it is not literally "nothing from $STAR".

  WITHOUT those options, ./StarDb does NOT simply win, and this is the most
  misunderstood thing here, so it is measured and quoted rather than asserted:

    * all .C files from EVERY search directory are pooled, and
      St_db_Maker::FindLeft() takes the one whose timestamp is LATEST at or
      before the requested time. Which directory it came from is irrelevant.
    * where a file and a MySQL row both exist, St_db_Maker.cxx:827 decides:
          case 3:   // Both SQL and CINT objects
            if (valsCINT[0].Get()>=valsSQL[0].Get()) { kase = 2; ... } // file
            else                                     { kase = 1; ... } // MySQL
      again the LATER beginTime wins. Files do not categorically beat the DB.
    * the list StBFChain builds is MySQL, $STAR/StarDb, $STAR/obj/StarDb,
      $PWD/StarDb, $PWD/obj/StarDb -- $PWD/StarDb is FOURTH. Position in that
      list is not precedence.

    MEASURED 2026-10-13 on FST, requesting a Run 22 date with ./StarDb holding
    the renamed tables:
        with $STAR/StarDb in the list   ->  RELEASE won  (+0.07126,-0.30102)
        with NoMySQLDb,NoStarCintDb     ->  OURS won     (+0.14876,-0.31702)
    The first is why the options matter: our files are stamped 20211015.000001
    and the release ships 20211110.000001, which is LATER and therefore wins, so
    edits to the local copies are silently ignored. (The resulting geometry is
    the same either way here -- the two decompositions sum to the same
    translation -- but an edit does nothing, with no warning.)

    If you cannot pass the options, the other way out is a timestamp later than
    every other entry: copy the file you want to <table>.20211113.000000.C in
    ./StarDb and edit that.

  * REMEMBER TO REMOVE IT when you want the database values back.

  * CONDOR JOBS run in their own scratch directory, so ./StarDb must exist
    THERE, not where you submitted from. Copy or link the directory into the
    job's working/tmp directory too, and pass the same options, or the job
    quietly uses something else and your test shows no change.


What each timestamp means
-------------------------
Translations are cm; no rotation has ever been non-identity. Only fstOnTpc and
hssOnFst are ever non-identity -- fstWedgeOnHss and fstSensorOnWedge are
identity in every version to date.

IMPORTANT: none of these is an alignment iteration. Every non-identity number
here is THE SURVEY. $STAR/StarDb/Geometry/fst ships it as hssOnFst at
20211110.000001:

      Left  half  t = (+0.07126, -0.30102, 0) cm
      Right half  t = (+0.22626, -0.33302, 0) cm

i.e. the FST halves sit 3.0-3.3 mm low in y and 0.7/2.3 mm off in x -- the same
order as the DB beamline y0 = -2.2 mm. FST alignment has never been iterated
past the survey. If and when it is, add a new timestamp; do not edit these.

Filenames are Gene's slots. His rule is kept at

      https://www.star.bnl.gov/protected/spin/akio/fcs/database_timestamp.txt

Per run year it reserves an "ideal" date and a "survey/estimate" date, both
before the run starts, with constants measured from real data going any time
after. The first
entry at a date uses time 000000, and later revisions of the same numbers step
the time.

  REAL FILES -- Run 22, where the survey was taken
      20211010.000000   identity, all four tables.  Run 22 IDEAL slot. Also
                        serves as "misalignment off", and as the floor
                        St_db_Maker needs (see FindLeft note below).
      20211015.000000   the survey as the release ships it, all of it in
                        hssOnFst.  Run 22 SURVEY slot.
      20211015.000001   THE SAME SURVEY, re-decomposed: common part lifted into
                        fstOnTpc (+0.14876, -0.31702), symmetric residual left
                        in hssOnFst (-/+0.07750, +/-0.01600).
                        Run 22 SURVEY slot, revision 1.  THIS IS WHAT WE RUN.

  The last two are not successive measurements and carry no new information:
  fstOnTpc x hssOnFst gives exactly the same total for both halves either way,
  verified element by element. The re-decomposed form exists because the common
  offset belongs in fstOnTpc physically -- the whole detector sits low, plus a
  small differential between the halves -- and because an FST-global shift is
  then one number to iterate instead of two. Push one of them, not both.

  SYMLINKS -- the same survey carried into the later run years
      20230410.000000 -> 20211010.000000     Run 23 ideal
      20230415.000000 -> 20211015.000001     Run 23 survey
      20231210.000000 -> 20211010.000000     Run 24 ideal
      20231215.000000 -> 20211015.000001     Run 24 survey
      20241210.000000 -> 20211010.000000     Run 25 ideal
      20241215.000000 -> 20211015.000001     Run 25 survey

  Symlinks, not copies, so there is one copy of every number and the later
  years cannot silently drift from Run 22.

  They are not strictly required for the local path: St_db_Maker::FindLeft()
  takes the latest entry at or before the requested time, per table and per
  namespace, so a Run 24 job resolves to the Run 22 survey anyway. They exist
  so each run year has an explicit entry in Gene's scheme instead of
  inheriting one by accident, and so the DB shows that intent. If a year ever
  gets its own FST alignment, replace that year's symlink with a real file.

  * FILENAME COLLISION, now resolved. Before the rename our identity file was
    called 20211110.000001 -- the same name the release uses for its SURVEY --
    so dropping this directory into ./StarDb replaced the release survey with
    identity under the same name. The rename to 20211010.000000 removes that
    collision. The release file still exists, though, and $STAR/StarDb beats
    MySQL; see "Pushing to the database".

  The "comment" field reads "Identity Matrix" in all of these files, including
  the non-identity ones. It is stale, and memcpy(&row.comment,"...",1) copies a
  single byte anyway, so the field carries no usable information. Do not rely
  on it; this README is the record.


The "misalign" BFC option is what makes any of this take effect
---------------------------------------------------------------
The AGML/XML geometry reads these survey tables ONLY when the bfc "misalign"
option is on. Its entry in BigFullChain.h is

    {"misalign", "", "", "", "", "-AgMLideal", "Misaligned Geometry", kFALSE},

i.e. it works by taking AgMLideal OUT of the chain, which is what lets
AgPosition apply the <Misalign> entries in the geometry XML. Without it the
tables are ignored completely and the geometry is built ideal, however
carefully you edited them -- no warning, no log line, the numbers simply do not
matter.

With "misalign" on, these tables feed BOTH of:

  * fGeom.root generation. build_geom_misalign.C passes misalign=1, so the
    cached geometry is built FROM these tables. The cache is a frozen snapshot:
    once built it no longer consults the tables at all.

  * MC / simulation. The simulated detector is placed with these same
    parameters, so changing a table changes where simulated hits land, not just
    how real hits are reconstructed.

COPYING IS AS DANGEROUS AS LINKING. Both are, for different reasons, and the
copy is the one that actually catches people out:

  * a LINK means someone else's edit, or your own later edit, silently changes
    every job that resolves through it -- reconstruction, geometry building and
    MC alike -- including jobs you meant as a fixed reference.

  * a COPY gives no hint it exists. A symlink at least announces itself in
    "ls -l"; a copied directory looks like an ordinary directory and nothing
    about it says "this is shadowing the release and the database". It also
    goes stale the moment macro/ or the DB is updated, and it keeps winning
    anyway. Weeks later the numbers disagree with everyone else's and the
    reason is a directory you forgot you made.

The real hazard is not copy-versus-link, it is that ./StarDb/Geometry/* EXISTS
AT ALL and silently takes precedence. There is no log line to catch it:
St_db_Maker does not report which of the three sources a table came from, and
StFttDb only says whether it used survey tables, not where they came from
(StFstDb does not even say that much). So check by hand before you trust a
result:

      ls -ld ./StarDb/Geometry/* 2>/dev/null

which prints nothing if you are clean, "d..." if you copied, and the "-> ..."
arrow if you linked. Run it in the job's working directory too, not only where
you submitted from.

Habits that work: keep one working directory per alignment study rather than
editing in place, and delete or rename ./StarDb the moment the study is over,
instead of leaving it for next time.

And be equally careful about WHICH fGeom.root you are using. The cache and the
tables are two separate things that can disagree, and nothing checks that they
match. A stale fGeom built from older tables quietly keeps using the old
alignment while ./StarDb shows the new numbers -- which looks exactly like "the
new alignment had no effect". If you change a table, rebuild the cache, and
name it so the versions are distinguishable: that is what the sdt and label
arguments of build_geom_misalign.C are for.


Proving it is safe to remove the release copy
---------------------------------------------
Do not delete $STAR/StarDb/Geometry/* and hope. The chain can simulate the
removal first, because StBFChain honours these options (BigFullChain.h:1159):

      NoStarCintDb     drops $STAR/StarDb   <- simulates the removal
      NoLocalCintDb    drops $PWD/StarDb
      NoCintDb         drops both, leaving MySQL only
      NoMySQLDb        drops MySQL

So after pushing, and BEFORE opening the removal PR:

  1. run the chain with NoCintDb, i.e. MySQL only, and confirm the survey comes
     back with the numbers you pushed. MIND THE TIMESTAMP: until the 2021-10-25
     entries are deactivated, a real-data timestamp still resolves to those
     identity rows and this check fails for the wrong reason. Use a
     survey-window date (sdt20211019) before deactivation and a real-data date
     after it -- see "What is already in the database" above;
  2. rebuild a geometry cache the same way and check it against the current one
     -- the translations should be identical, since the DB entries hold the same
     survey;
  3. only then propose deleting the files from the repository.

That sequence is also the evidence the removal PR needs: "with NoCintDb the
geometry is unchanged, so the release copy is redundant" is reviewable, whereas
"we pushed it, trust us" is not.

What is already in the database, and why it has to be deactivated
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Checked 2026-10-15. Geometry_fst already holds eleven entries, all dated
2021-10-25, loaded in 2022:

      table               beginTime              rows   entryTime
      fstOnTpc            2021-10-25 00:00:11      1    2022-01-15 16:44:13
      fstOnTpc            2021-10-25 00:00:12      1    2022-01-15 16:46:38
      fstOnTpc            2021-10-25 00:00:13      1    2022-01-15 16:50:45
      fstOnTpc            2021-10-25 00:00:14      1    2022-01-15 17:55:55
      hssOnFst            2021-10-25 00:00:00      2    2022-01-15 17:42:50
      fstWedgeOnHss       2021-10-25 00:00:00     36    2022-01-15 17:49:10
      fstSensorOnWedge    2021-10-25 00:00:00    108    2022-01-15 17:53:09
      fstSensorOnWedge    2021-10-25 00:00:01     36    2022-07-26 18:24:13
      fstSensorOnWedge    2021-10-25 00:00:02     36    2022-07-26 18:27:21
      fstSensorOnWedge    2021-10-25 00:00:03    108    2022-07-26 18:28:11
      fstSensorOnWedge    2021-10-25 00:00:04    108    2022-07-26 18:51:09

All flavor "ofl", all deactive=0, 438 rows in total. Note the two
fstSensorOnWedge entries with 36 rows where 108 is right -- malformed, but
superseded by 00:00:03 and 00:00:04.

EVERY ONE OF THEM IS IDENTITY. Measured, not assumed: t=(0,0,0) on every row,
r00=r11=r22=1 and all six off-diagonals 0 on every row, in all four tables. So
the FST survey has never been in the database for real data -- every Run 22
real-data job to date has been reconstructed with ideal FST geometry.

Re-check it with:

      mysql -h star-db01.sdcc.bnl.gov -P 3316 -u loader -N -e \
        "select beginTime, min(entryTime), count(*),
                sum(abs(t0)+abs(t1)+abs(t2)>1e-9) as nonzero_translation
         from Geometry_fst.fstOnTpc group by beginTime order by beginTime;"

WHY THIS MATTERS. 2021-10-25 sits INSIDE Gene's "real data: any time after
that" window, i.e. after the 2021-10-10 ideal and 2021-10-15 survey slots.
St_db_Maker takes the latest entry at or before the requested time, so for any
real-data timestamp these identity rows win over anything we write at
2021-10-15. Our entries would be reachable only by MC timestamps.

TWO WAYS OUT, AND WHY THE SECOND ONE IS RIGHT

  (a) Supersede: write the survey a second time at a beginTime after
      2021-10-25 00:00:14, e.g. 2021-10-26 00:00:00. Non-destructive, needs no
      admin, works immediately.

      But it is wrong as a standing arrangement. It makes TWO entries the
      permanent requirement for every future update: one in the survey window
      (2021-10-15..10-25) for MC, one after 2021-10-25 for real data. The next
      alignment iteration -- Xihe's, or ours -- has to remember both. Forget
      either one and MC and real data silently run on different geometry, with
      no error and no log line saying so. That is the same class of trap as
      ./StarDb shadowing, and it recurs every single time.

  (b) DEACTIVATE the eleven entries (set deactive != 0; the rows stay, nothing
      is deleted, and it can be undone). Then the newest entry at or before any
      real-data timestamp is the 2021-10-15 survey, and from then on ONE entry
      per update serves both MC and real data:

          2021-10-14  ->  ideal22   (2021-10-10)    MC, misalignment off
          2021-10-19  ->  survey22  (2021-10-15)    MC, survey
          any later   ->  survey22  (2021-10-15)    real data
          next iteration goes in once, at 2021-10-15 00:00:02 or later

      This is the plan. It needs a request to the DB team (Gene / Dmitry) --
      storeDbTable cannot deactivate, only insert.

NOTHING IS LOST BY DEACTIVATING. The content of those entries is identity, and
identity is exactly what our ideal22 entry at 2021-10-10 provides. Anyone who
genuinely wants the old behaviour -- reproducing a Run 22 production that was
reconstructed without the survey -- asks for a timestamp in
[2021-10-10, 2021-10-15) and gets the same numbers. Reproducibility survives;
only the default changes. Deactivation also leaves the rows visible in the DB,
so the history stays readable.

ORDER OF OPERATIONS. Push first, deactivate second, never the other way round:

  1. push our slots (ideal22 first -- it is also the floor St_db_Maker needs);
  2. verify with NoCintDb using a SURVEY-window timestamp, e.g. sdt20211019,
     which resolves below 2021-10-25 and so already reads our entry;
  3. request deactivation of the eleven entries above;
  4. re-verify with NoCintDb using a REAL-DATA timestamp -- this is the step
     that can only pass after (3);
  5. only then the release-file removal PR.

Between 1 and 3 nothing changes for anybody: real data keeps resolving to the
identity rows, exactly as today. If the order were reversed there would be a
window with no Geometry/fst entry at all below a real-data timestamp.

AND IT CHANGES WHAT EVERYONE GETS. Step 3 is the moment the survey becomes the
default for new Run 22 real-data jobs -- Xihe's alignment work, Daniel's, any
production re-run. It is the intended effect and it is the right geometry, but
it is not ours alone to spring on people: tell Gene and Daniel before asking
for the deactivation, not after.


Pushing to the database
-----------------------
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey22")'            dry run
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey22","writedb")'  store one slot
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("all","writedb")'       store every slot

Dry run first -- it prints every row it would write and touches nothing. Pass a
bad tag to list them all. "all" walks the eight slots in order, skipping
survey22flat, which is redundant with survey22.

Tags: ideal22 survey22flat survey22 ideal23 survey23 ideal24 survey24
      ideal25 survey25

An optional third argument restricts the push to ONE of the four tables:

      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal22","writedb","fstOnTpc")'

That exists only for the first push, to test write permission on the smallest
possible change. A partial update leaves the geometry inconsistent at that
timestamp -- StFstDb::loadGeometry() chains all four tables -- so finish the
other three straight away.


FIRST PUSH -- testing write permission
--------------------------------------
Nothing here has ever been written to the database, so test with the smallest,
least consequential thing first: ideal22 / fstOnTpc. One row, identity content,
beginTime 2021-10-10, which is BELOW every entry that currently exists
(2021-10-25), so even a completely successful push cannot change what any job
reads today. It is also the first thing we want in the database anyway -- it is
the floor St_db_Maker needs.

  1. push one table of one tag

      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal22","writedb","fstOnTpc")'

     On success the log says: stored fstOnTpc at 2021-10-10 00:00:00
     If write permission is missing, StDbManager reports it and nothing is
     written -- the dry run has already proved the file reading works, so any
     failure at this point is the database side.

  2. confirm it landed, independently of the macro

      mysql -h star-db01.sdcc.bnl.gov -P 3316 -u loader -N -e \
        "select beginTime, entryTime, dataID, nodeID, elementID, flavor,
                deactive, Id, t0, t1, t2, r00, r11, r22
           from Geometry_fst.fstOnTpc where beginTime='2021-10-10 00:00:00';"

     Expect exactly ONE row: entryTime = now, flavor ofl, deactive 0,
     t=(0,0,0), r00=r11=r22=1.

  3. then the other three tables of the same tag, one at a time

      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal22","writedb","hssOnFst")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal22","writedb","fstWedgeOnHss")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal22","writedb","fstSensorOnWedge")'

     and check 2 / 36 / 108 rows respectively at 2021-10-10 00:00:00.

  4. then the remaining seven tags, each in full. Do NOT use "all" at this
     point -- it would redo ideal22:

      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey22","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal23","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey23","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal24","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey24","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("ideal25","writedb")'
      root4star -b -q 'fstGeometry_db.C("survey25","writedb")'

  NEVER STORE THE SAME (table, beginTime) TWICE. The primary key of these
  tables is (nodeID, elementID, beginTime, flavor, deactive), so a second store
  at the same beginTime collides with the first -- it cannot quietly become a
  duplicate entry, but whether it errors or overwrites has not been tested
  here, and there is no reason to find out. That is also why every revision in
  the existing database stepped the time by a second (00:00:11, :12, :13, :14)
  rather than rewriting in place.

  Final check, all four tables:

      for t in fstOnTpc hssOnFst fstWedgeOnHss fstSensorOnWedge ; do
        mysql -h star-db01.sdcc.bnl.gov -P 3316 -u loader -N -e \
          "select '$t', beginTime, count(*) from Geometry_fst.$t
             group by beginTime order by beginTime;"
      done

     Expect 8 new beginTimes per table (2021-10-10, 2021-10-15, 2023-04-10,
     2023-04-15, 2023-12-10, 2023-12-15, 2024-12-10, 2024-12-15) with
     1 / 2 / 36 / 108 rows, alongside the old 2021-10-25 ones.

  Then: deactivation (see "What is already in the database" above and
  deactivate.txt, which is the request to send to Dmitry), and only after that
  the release-file removal PR.

PUSHING IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. St_db_Maker resolves ./StarDb, then
$STAR/StarDb, then MySQL, and $STAR/StarDb/Geometry/fst still holds the
release's four files at 20211110.000001. Those beat anything in the database.
Until they are removed from the STAR repository, production keeps reading the
release copy and never sees these entries. That removal is a separate PR and
the last step of this migration -- the push is not done until it has landed.

After the push and that removal:
  - rebuild any fGeom.*.root whose sdt no longer resolves to the entry you
    intend. The cache is a frozen snapshot and nothing checks it against the
    tables (see "The misalign option" above);
  - these files stay here as the record of what was pushed, and as what to copy
    into ./StarDb when you want to override it.
